Deborah Gough
November 12, 2006
THE right to a "fair go" is the thing almost all Australians put at the top of their list when it comes to values.
A survey released today shows 91 per cent of people believe a fair go is important, with most listing the need for rights to welfare, housing and indigenous reconciliation to make the country fairer.
The poll was less than conclusive about whether Australia was getting fairer for all, with 45 per cent saying it was not.
There also appears to be a divide between younger and older people �� 60 per cent of those aged 14-35 believed the country was getting fairer but the over 35s were more pessimistic.
Curiously though, the survey also found 77 per cent believed the gap between rich and poor was widening.
The Roy Morgan survey for the Australian Council of Social Service had sought to find some "agreed principles" in the nation as well as an understanding of perceptions of how well Australians felt the country was doing as a whole.
Asked to nominate 10 essentials to make Australia fairer, respondents listed fair rights and responsibilities, welfare, education, environment, work, health, housing, services, community (including safety) and reconciliation.
ACOSS executive director Andrew Johnson said the random poll asked people to imagine how to make Australia fairer in good economic times.
"I guess what's pretty heartening is that the faith we have in Australia exists and they listed all of these things that would be of a benefit to everyone rather than just to themselves," Mr Johnson said.
The survey found 82 per cent believed it was government's responsibility to make the country fairer.
Mr Johnson called for governments to respond to public perceptions by introducing "fair go" impact statements for all new legislation as well as a strategic approach to poverty.
Australia Institute executive director Clive Hamilton said the figures could be interpreted to be a greater tolerance for inequality.
Dr Hamilton said the rise of "middle-class welfare" for services such as child care and health insurance in affluent times meant that Australians might feel the country was fairer generally because they personally were wealthier. But coupled with the notion of a widening gap between rich and poor, it meant Australians felt "hard done by" because they were not as wealthy as the very rich.
"People in Australia have been extensively preoccupied by their own monetary wealth and feel that they don't actually have a lot of money," he said.
"There is a lot of imagined deprivation and not a lot of real deprivation in the middle classes and it is an evil that we need to overcome."{r}
