Somehow I found myself the other night in a small office in the shadow of Disneyland at a Mary Kay meeting. It happens.
It is in such settings, though, where you can meet some of the most fascinating people and, if you aren��t in too much of hurry, learn a thing or five.
It seems I had given a few bucks last fall to the group��s Adopt-A-Grandparent program, through which it buys items for the elderly in nursing homes; the items will be wrapped and presented to them on Christmas Eve. Trust me, it is impossible to turn down such a request when it is presented to you.
On this night, though, the Mary Kay Dream Studio in Anaheim to which I had given my donation was being honored by the Orange County Council on Aging with its ��Betty Bartley Angel with a Heart Award,�� recognizing its outsized assistance with its Christmas program.
The group had raised enough money to put together nearly 700 gifts for Orange County seniors, who otherwise would have been completely forgotten during the holidays.
I am a sucker for such stories. I think it goes back to my own mother, God rest her, who spent her final years in a nursing home. I figure I would have wanted someone to do the same for her.
It is why, too, I have spent a lot of time over the years in and writing about elderly care facilities. Going all the way back to Mom, it is almost always the same.
You walk in the front door and often are greeted by an array of men and women in wheelchairs or steadying themselves on walkers. You must almost walk gingerly as you pass them. And almost always, one or two of the residents will lift their sad eyes to yours and ask so hopefully: ��Are you here to see me?��
You have got to be one hardened, almost cold-hearted individual not to cry and hug them right there.
The awards festivities were over when I sidled up to Lisa Wright Jenkins.
She is the president and chief executive of the Orange County Council on Aging. She had told the group that with its help, the Council last year delivered more than 2,700 Christmas presents to the elderly through its SmileMakers Guild program.
I had questions.
She minces no words when talking about what she and the council do, puts it all right out there.
There are more than 28,000 people in 1,100 residential care facilities in Orange County, she said, and about half of them have no family or friends actively involved in their care.
Think about that.
I have. It tells me that it is possible to live your entire life, doing all the things we humans do, yet in the final chapter, in the dimming years of that life, you can find yourself all alone, depending on strangers in white uniforms to get you out of bed, to feed you, to put you back in bed at night.
��It is a real challenge in Orange County,�� Wright Jenkins said. ��There are people who have been literally forgotten among us.��
It is why the Council began the SmileMakers Guild. Cathy Johnson is the holiday project co-chair.
��You change their life. You brighten their day,�� she told me. ��You know, somebody remembered them, even a stranger at the holidays.��
She had the time two years ago to get involved, Johnston said. It was also a way of honoring her grandmother, who at the time was living in Missouri.
The men and women of the council��s ombudsmen program, who make year-round announced and unannounced visits to residential care facilities to check on the welfare of their clients, canvass the facilities for what the men and women might want at Christmas time.
A list is prepared, and a cardboard gold angel is made for each recipient. Wright Jenkins ran out to show me one she had in her car.
Maria F., 85, the sticker on the back of the angel read, wanted simply a lap blanket. She got one.
��Most of the requests are for things like pajamas, a sweat suit, slippers, sometimes a pink sweater or a puzzle,�� Johnston explains.
��It is never anything like electronics, a computer. And the letters we receive from them – oh, my goodness. It is just a simple gift left on a bed. To them, other than a phone call from a son, they say it is the only gift they got. To them, it means there are still good-hearted people in this life.��
Doesn��t all or any of it ever really get to you? I asked Wright Jenkins.
��Here in Orange County,�� she replied, ��seniors are suffering. We try to alleviate it, and put the spotlight on those men and women who built this county, let them know they are remembered.��
The Council this year is celebrating its 40th anniversary. With its more than 400 volunteers, it now serves 146,000 Orange County seniors and their families.
��My first job was as a candy-striper in a nursing home, so I guess I��ve always been drawn to this,�� Wright Jenkins said.
��It��s easy to forget the elderly. You see children and pets on the street all the time. Never the elderly. So I want to shed a light on them and help them.��
It is the kind of help that makes{r} you{g} want{g} to{g} cry{g}.{r}
