Delaying catch-up payments to Bridgeport's pension funds may help the city temporarily, but it will likely put it deeper in the hole later on.
The House and Senate have passed an amendment letting Bridgeport put off some of its pension fund payments for six years because the city is struggling with a deficit. (See Mayor Joe Ganim's letter today.) Sen. Ed Gomes, D-Bridgeport, argued, paradoxically, that "what we need is a decrease in the payments in order to catch up to what we owe."
There's no guarantee Bridgeport will one day be able to replenish those funds, however, despite its promise that it will be in better financial shape down the road.
