All the works are tabletop sculptures of about 2 square feet made of papier-mache. Each began as a casting of an inflated beach ball the artist crushed, cut and repeatedly added to before arriving at a final form that he painted.
The pieces are horizontally disposed, though two of the eight also are narrow and tall, one pronouncedly so. The others meander or sprawl. Both the surfaces (lumpy) and colors (odd) underline a sense of casualness. The forms took shape as the artist worked rather than followed a plan he predetermined. There are no preparatory drawings.
Each sculpture is continuously undulating, giving a succession of ledges, crevices, voids and folds. Fecteau's painting in one, two or three colors emphasizes the unpredictability of the works' wavelike movements. The overall shapes, which frequently relate hard edges to billows, are so unpredictable that no single view of a sculpture can reveal it all. Each has to be seen from multiple viewpoints, in the round.
